WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.809
Terraform keeps a memory of what it made.

2
00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:09.071
It is a JSON file called terraform.tfstate.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.757
Its main job is a map.

4
00:00:10.757 --> 00:00:13.340
On the left, an address from your code.

5
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.851
On the right, the real ID of the object it points to.

6
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:22.129
So docker_container.web is the container with this ID, over in Docker.

7
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:26.071
Why not just ask Docker which containers exist?

8
00:00:26.071 --> 00:00:29.391
Because Docker cannot tell which ones this folder owns.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.788
State is the list of what this folder owns, with the ID of each.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.404
Open it with jq, and you find a few top-level keys.

11
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:40.463
version is the file format.

12
00:00:40.463 --> 00:00:43.988
serial counts how many times this state was written.

13
00:00:43.988 --> 00:00:47.784
lineage is an ID given when the state was first created.

14
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.127
And resources holds every resource, with every attribute the provider reported.

15
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:55.580
Those attributes include secrets.

16
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:03.121
In a test, a variable marked sensitive went into a container as REDIS_PASSWORD equals s3cret-123.

17
00:01:03.121 --> 00:01:06.064
Plan and apply printed sensitive value.

18
00:01:06.064 --> 00:01:08.705
terraform output printed sensitive.

19
00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:11.461
But the state file held it in plain text.

20
00:01:11.461 --> 00:01:14.728
Anyone who can read your state can read your secrets.

21
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:18.014
So treat the file like a password file.

22
00:01:18.014 --> 00:01:20.750
Never commit it, and never edit it by hand.

23
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:22.849
Read it through commands instead.

24
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:25.952
By default, state is a local file.

25
00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:30.656
That breaks as soon as a second person, or a pipeline, runs Terraform.

26
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.567
Two copies drift apart, and nothing stops two applies from running at once.

27
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.553
A backend decides where state is stored.

28
00:01:38.553 --> 00:01:42.064
A remote backend keeps one copy that everyone uses.

29
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:47.159
Most backends also lock it, so only one run at a time can change the state.

30
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:51.708
Start an apply in one terminal, and a plan in another.

31
00:01:51.708 --> 00:01:55.149
The second stops with: Error acquiring the state lock.

32
00:01:55.367 --> 00:02:02.975
On S3, use_lockfile set to true keeps that small .tflock file next to the state, while a run holds the lock.

33
00:02:02.975 --> 00:02:06.708
And bucket versioning lets you roll back a bad write.

34
00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:08.182
Now drift.

35
00:02:08.182 --> 00:02:14.992
Drift is any difference between the real world and Terraform's state, usually from a change made by hand.

36
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.537
Someone runs docker update by hand, and sets restart to always.

37
00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:24.504
plan -refresh-only shows only the changes made outside Terraform.

38
00:02:24.504 --> 00:02:26.180
It proposes no actions.

39
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.314
apply -refresh-only writes those changes into the state.

40
00:02:30.314 --> 00:02:32.131
It changes no real object.

41
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:35.413
People expect that to make the change permanent.

42
00:02:35.413 --> 00:02:36.183
It does not.

43
00:02:36.183 --> 00:02:40.609
The code still says no, so the next normal plan wants to put no back.

44
00:02:41.433 --> 00:02:44.527
So after drift, you decide which side is right.

45
00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:49.859
If the code is right, run a normal apply, and Terraform undoes the manual change.

46
00:02:49.859 --> 00:02:53.677
If reality is right, change the code to match, then apply.

47
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.506
Back to the map.

48
00:02:55.506 --> 00:03:01.980
Your code has a container resource labelled web, and the state maps that address to the real container.

49
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.532
Rename the label, from web to site.

50
00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:08.393
To Terraform, you deleted one resource and wrote a new one.

51
00:03:08.393 --> 00:03:14.347
The plan wants to destroy the container, and build a new one, to change a name only you can see.

52
00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.524
A moved block fixes that.

53
00:03:16.524 --> 00:03:20.596
From docker_container.web, to docker_container.site.

54
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.338
Now the plan says web has moved to site.

55
00:03:24.338 --> 00:03:27.643
Zero to add, zero to change, zero to destroy.

56
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:30.875
Apply, and the state uses the new address.

57
00:03:30.875 --> 00:03:32.595
Same container, same ID.

58
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.865
Sometimes the real object comes first.

59
00:03:35.865 --> 00:03:39.562
Someone started a container by hand, called state-legacy.

60
00:03:39.562 --> 00:03:41.313
The state does not know it.

61
00:03:41.533 --> 00:03:43.293
An import block adopts it.

62
00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:44.849
It takes two arguments.

63
00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:47.015
to is the address it should get.

64
00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:49.566
id is the provider's ID for it.

65
00:03:49.566 --> 00:03:53.780
For a Docker container, the full ID from docker inspect.

66
00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.360
A name does not work.

67
00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:59.513
You also write the matching resource block yourself, then plan.

68
00:03:59.513 --> 00:04:01.079
Read that plan with care.

69
00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:06.467
If your block differs from the real object, the plan imports it, and then replaces it.

70
00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.803
So you adjust the code until the plan imports, and destroys nothing.

71
00:04:11.033 --> 00:04:15.182
Apply, and the state gains a row, with the container's own ID.

72
00:04:15.182 --> 00:04:17.122
Then delete the import block.

73
00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:18.861
It has nothing left to do.

74
00:04:19.667 --> 00:04:24.461
State maps each address to a real ID, and it holds secrets, so guard it.

75
00:04:24.461 --> 00:04:26.792
Keep it in a locked remote backend.

76
00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:32.785
And moved, import and refresh-only each fix one link, in a plan you read before you apply.
